<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Great Perm</h1><p class="st-meta">1451 CE – 1505 CE (approx.) · capital Cherdyn</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Komi principality on the upper Kama, Christianised by Saint Stephen of Perm and absorbed into Muscovy in 1505.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-permyaks">Komi-Permyak</a> lands on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kama-river">Kama</a> were long a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Novgorod</a>. In the late 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-of-perm">Saint Stephen of Perm</a> converted many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komi</a> and created the Old Permic alphabet for their language. By the mid 15th century the land was ruled by local princes dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherdyn">Cherdyn</a> as their centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Great Perm was a land of forests, fur trapping and salt, linking the Russian north with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Its princes, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-ermolaevich">Mikhail Ermolaevich</a>, were baptised and served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> while ruling local communities. In 1472 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> sent an army that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherdyn">Cherdyn</a> and tightened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> control.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Local princely rule lasted until 1505, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> replaced Prince Matvey Mikhailovich with its own governor. The region became a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stroganov-family">Stroganov family</a> and for Russian expansion across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/great-perm.jpg" alt="Great Perm" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Alexander V. Solomin · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Perm_and_Pelym_principalities_RU.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1451 CE – 1505 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cherdyn</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mikhail Ermolaevich, Matvey Mikhailovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Komi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Great Perm</dd></dl></aside></div>
